}

/// **A known defect, pinned rather than fixed.**
///
/// `docs/experiments/0022` found this and deliberately left it uncorrected: the tier names are
/// matched case-insensitively against bare words, so the ordinary English adjective in *"a stated
/// prerequisite"* is read as the STATED evidence tier. `UNKNOWN … and … stated` then looks like
/// two tiers on one claim, and a sound answer fails the service-wide prefilter every case runs
/// through. One of the twenty answers in that probe used the bare word, and it is the one that
/// failed.
///
/// It is asserted here so the behaviour is visible and cannot be lost in a refactor. **Correcting
/// it needs the same treatment every grader change gets** — a rejected control proving the rule
/// still catches a real compound claim, an accepted control for the adjective, and a fresh run —
/// and it must not be done in response to a run it would change.
#[test]
fn known_defect_0022_a_bare_stated_is_read_as_an_evidence_tier() {
    let sound = "No record shows sample export was supplied — **UNKNOWN**, this was explicitly \
requested and is a stated prerequisite for technical review.";
    assert!(
        compound_tier_claim(sound).is_some(),
        "0022's defect is gone — if that was deliberate, delete this test and say so in an \
         experiment; if not, something narrowed the rule by accident"
    );
    // The same sentence with one word changed clears it, which is what makes the cause certain.
    let reworded = sound.replace("a stated prerequisite", "a documented prerequisite");
    assert_eq!(compound_tier_claim(&reworded), None, "only the bare adjective triggers it");
}

